 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nOAuth1 verifier validation returns incorrect result\n\n#### Description\n\nThe `validate_verifier` method in `RequestValidator` is not working as expected. Instead of properly validating the verifier against the stored value, it appears to be returning the opposite result - rejecting valid verifiers and accepting invalid ones.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom oauthlib.oauth1.rfc5849.request_validator import RequestValidator\n\nclass TestValidator(RequestValidator):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        # Store a known verifier for testing\n        self.stored_verifiers = {\n            ('client_key', 'request_token'): 'valid_verifier_123'\n        }\n    \n    def get_client_secret(self, client_key, request):\n        return 'client_secret'\n    \n    def get_request_token_secret(self, client_key, token, request):\n        return 'token_secret'\n\nvalidator = TestValidator()\n\n# This should return True for a valid verifier but returns False\nresult_valid = validator.validate_verifier('valid_verifier_123', 'client_key', 'request_token', None)\nprint(f\"Valid verifier result: {result_valid}\")  # Expected: True, Actual: False\n\n# This should return False for an invalid verifier but returns True  \nresult_invalid = validator.validate_verifier('wrong_verifier', 'client_key', 'request_token', None)\nprint(f\"Invalid verifier result: {result_invalid}\")  # Expected: False, Actual: True\n```\n\nThe method seems to be inverting the validation logic, causing OAuth1 authorization flows to fail when they should succeed and vice versa.\n</issue>\n",
